  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe Nor'easter will slowly move up the coast this afternoon and \ntonight. This will lead to showers becoming more isolated this \nafternoon and through the evening. As the system pulls away, showers \nwill come to an end with high pressure gradually building in. Model \nsoundings show low-level moisture under an inversion. Most profiles \nwould favor low stratus over fog through both will be possible \nacross the region tonight. Where fog does develop, it may become \ndense. \n\nThe low stratus and fog will clear up by the late morning hours on \nTuesday. Persistent northerly flow will keep some moisture around \nand cloudy skies will likely linger well into the afternoon before \ndrier air moves in from the north. Rain is not expected tomorrow but \na couple of models still try to pop up a couple pockets of what \nwould just be drizzle. Confidence was too low to include any \nmentionable PoPs in this forecast update. Without the rain and some \nlate breaks in cloud cover, temperatures will climb into the 60s \nacross the region. Clearing skies overnight will lead to good \nradiational cooling and fog development. There is potential for fog \nto become widespread across the region. \n\nA ridge builds into the region Wednesday. Southwesterly flow will \nadvect in warmer air, helping push temperatures into the 70s and \neven 80s through Thursday. Nighttime temperatures will be more mild \nas well, only falling into the 50s and 60s. Stronger flow aloft on \nThursday will have potential to mix down leading to gusts of 15 to \n20 mph. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next chance for rain across the region comes with a frontal \nboundary late in the week. This system approaches the region \nThursday night and begins to slowly move through on Friday. The \nfront eventually departs the region on Saturday with high pressure \nand cooler air filling in behind. The high pressure quickly slides \noff to the east, allowing for a low pressure system to move up into \nthe Northeast on Sunday. Additional showers are expected with this \nsystem to round out the weekend. Unlike this week's Nor'easter, this \nlow pressure system will be quicker to move through with potential \nfor drier conditions to start next week.\n\nWith both of these systems, there are uncertainties. The arrival of \nthe frontal system still varies by 6 to 12 hours between guidance. \nModels have also trended slightly slower with this system. While \nguidance have come into better agreement with the timing of Sunday's \nsystem, there is still uncertainty on the track and how it interacts \nwith a low in Canada.",
